_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 8
---
order: 8
---
short_label: I.8
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: If two triangles have the two sides equal to two sides respectively, and also have the base equal to the base, then they also have the angles equal which are contained by the equal straight lines.
----
proof:

Let {ABC} and {DEF} be two triangles having the two sides {AB} and {AC} equal to the two sides {DE} and {DF} respectively, namely {AB} equal to {DE} and {AC} equal to {DF}, and let them have the base {BC} equal to the base {EF}.

I say that the angle {BAC} also equals the angle {EDF}.

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="350" height="240" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="I.8 diagram" src="propI8.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
geomlib.init({
    canvasid: "canvas_0",
    background: "35,19,100",
    title: "I.8",
    elements: [
        // Target triangle DEF (free).
        "D;point;free;220,50",
        "E;point;free;200,200",
        "F;point;free;320,150",
        "DEF;polygon;triangle;D,E,F;0;0;black;random",
        // Triangle ABC, built CONGRUENT to DEF and drag-safe: BA = ED,
        // AC = DF, BC = EF. CircBA = circle(centre B, radius |ED|) and A
        // rides it (so BA = ED); CircAC, CircBC give AC = DF and BC = EF;
        // C is the far intersection of their common chord.
        "B;point;free;50,200",
        "CircBA;circle;radius;B,E,D;0;0;0;0",
        "A;point;circleSlider;CircBA,70,50",
        "CircAC;circle;radius;A,D,F;0;0;0;0",
        "CircBC;circle;radius;B,E,F;0;0;0;0",
        "C'C;line;bichord;CircAC,CircBC;0;0;0",
        "C;point;last;C'C",
        "ABC;polygon;triangle;A,B,C;0;0;black;random",
        // The I.7 "fall beside" hypothetical apex (revealed mid-proof): if
        // BA, AC did NOT coincide with ED, DF they would land here as EG,
        // GF — giving base EF two apexes (D and G) with equal corresponding
        // sides, which I.7 forbids.
        "G;point;free;280,65",
        "EG;line;connect;E,G",
        "FG;line;connect;F,G",
        // Zero-color hover targets for the prose's side references (the
        // triangle polygons give no per-edge hover).
        "AB;line;connect;A,B;0;0;0",
        "AC;line;connect;A,C;0;0;0",
        "BC;line;connect;B,C;0;0;0",
        "DE;line;connect;D,E;0;0;0",
        "DF;line;connect;D,F;0;0;0",
        "EF;line;connect;E,F;0;0;0",
        // Apex angle markers (the conclusion ∠BAC = ∠EDF). Hidden in the
        // static figure (#100), swept in on their slide; named to match the
        // prose tokens (no triangle BAC/EDF to collide with).
        "BAC;sector;angleMarker;A,B,C",
        "EDF;sector;angleMarker;D,E,F"
    ],
    // The hypothetical apex G is introduced mid-proof; everything else is a
    // given shown from slide 1. C is a derived point (point;last) so, like
    // I.4's C, it is not auto-unioned — list "C" in every slide's visible
    // set so its vertex label persists.
    deferDraggables: ["G"],
    aliases: {
        "BA": "AB",
        "ED": "DE",
        "GF": "FG"
    },
    slides: [
        { text: "Let {ABC} and {DEF} be two triangles having the two sides {AB} and {AC} equal to the two sides {DE} and {DF} respectively,",
          visible: ["ABC","DEF","C"],
          highlighted: ["AB","AC","DE","DF"] },
        { text: "namely {AB} equal to {DE} and {AC} equal to {DF}, and let them have the base {BC} equal to the base {EF}.",
